    source: "01-start-here/about-how-you-work.md",
    summary: "Twenty minutes if you move, in your first couple of days — longer is fine, and so is leaving",
    order: 50,
    words: 2936,
    body: "# About how you work\n\nTwenty minutes if you move, in your first couple of days — longer is fine, and so is leaving\nthings blank. Bring it to your first conversation with Alex.\n\n> **This is not a test and it isn't stored anywhere formal.** The whole ramp was designed\n> before anyone had met you, which means it's built on guesses. This is how we replace the\n> guesses with facts and change the plan where it's wrong.\n>\n> **Honest answers make the next twelve weeks better. Flattering ones make them worse** — if\n> you say you're comfortable with something you're not, we'll skip past it and you'll be\n> quietly stuck in week six.\n>\n> It pairs with [`../05-self-assessment/baseline.md`](../05-self-assessment/baseline.md),\n> which covers *what you can do*. This one is about *how you like to work*.\n\n**The levers are real.** One answer in section 1 gets your BigQuery access requested in week\none, long before anyone thinks you need it — that one has a lead time measured in weeks and it\nis the access that most often bites. One answer in section 2 can replace a week of reading\nwith a week of shadowing. There's a\ntable at the end — [what your answers actually change](#what-your-answers-actually-change) —\nlisting every lever this sheet controls. Read it first if you want to know why any of these\nquestions are being asked.\n\n---\n\n## How to fill this in\n\n- **Tick tables** by putting an `x` in the cell. Ranking tables take a number.\n- **Written answers** go under the `>` line. A sentence is plenty. Nobody is reading this for\n  style.\n- **Blank is a real answer.** It means \"no strong feeling,\" and that's useful too.\n- **If you only have ten minutes,** do sections 1, 2 and 3. Those are the three that change\n  the most.\n\n---\n\n## 1 · What you've actually touched\n\nNot what you studied — what you've genuinely used, even a little.\n\n| | Never | Poked at it | Used it properly |\n|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| Google Ads (or any ad platform) | | | |\n| Google Sheets / Excel — filtering, sorting, a pivot table | | | |\n| Any analytics tool (GA, Adobe, similar) | | | |\n| SQL, or any query language | | | |\n| A dashboard tool — Power BI, Tableau, Looker, similar | | | |\n| Working in a spreadsheet with more than a few thousand rows | | | |\n| An AI assistant, for real work rather than curiosity | | | |\n\n**The spreadsheet row matters more than it looks.** Nearly every project in the ladder starts\nwith \"pull this report and sort it.\" If that part isn't second nature yet, say so — it's a\nhalf-day fix at the start and a constant tax if we miss it.\n\n**A \"Never\" in this table is not a problem. A hidden one is.** Here's what each one actually\ntriggers, so you can see that admitting to it buys you something:\n\n| If you've never touched | What changes |\n|---|---|\n| **SQL** | Your BigQuery access gets requested in week one regardless of when you'll use it — it goes through a different team and has the longest lead time of anything on your list. The SQL itself we teach later and slowly |\n| **Spreadsheets at scale** | We spend a session on it before Tier 1 rather than letting you discover it mid-project |\n| **Any ad platform** | T1-1 gets more time and Alex drives more of it. It's a paired project either way |\n| **A dashboard tool** | [The tools thread](../03-projects/the-tools-thread.md) starts earlier, since it's the file that explains which tool owns which number |\n| **AI tools** | [The AI thread](../03-projects/the-ai-thread.md) starts from the verifying habit rather than assuming it |\n\n**Anything else you've used that we haven't listed?**\n\n>\n\n---\n\n## 2 · How you learn best\n\nRank these 1–4, where 1 is what works best for you:\n\n| | Rank |\n|---|:---:|\n| Reading it and working it out | |\n| Watching someone do it, then doing it | |\n| Being told the concept out loud, then trying | |\n| Just doing it and asking when it breaks | |\n\n**This one genuinely changes what we build.** Almost everything we've written for you so far\nis *reading*, because that's what was easy to produce before we'd met you. If reading is\nthird or fourth on your list, that's a real problem with our plan and we'd rather find out in\nweek one than week six. The fix is not subtle — it's more paired sessions and more shadowing,\nwith the files demoted to lookup.\n\n**When you hit a concept that doesn't land, what do you normally do?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| Read it again, slower | |\n| Go and find a different explanation of the same thing | |\n| Skip it and come back once something else has clicked | |\n| Go looking for a concrete example to work through | |\n\nWorth knowing because of one file specifically:\n[`how-brady-measures.md`](../02-learning/how-brady-measures.md) is designed to be read three\ntimes, and your Day 8 is a deliberate second pass at it. If \"read it again, slower\" isn't how\nyour brain works, we'd rather build those passes differently than watch you grind at the same\npage.\n\n**Do you want the *why* before the *how*, or do you want to do the thing first and get the\nreasoning after?**\n\n>\n\n**Is there a course, video series or way of learning that's worked well for you before?**\n\n>\n\n---\n\n## 3 · When something isn't going well\n\n**When you get stuck on something, what do you usually do first?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| Ask straight away | |\n| Try for a bit, then ask | |\n| Keep going until I've cracked it or run out of day | |\n\nThere's no right answer, but there is a consequence: **the last one is the pattern we most\nwant to catch early**, because it's how a good week quietly becomes a lost one. If that's\nyou, say so — Alex will check in more often rather than waiting for you to raise a hand.\n\n**Where would you rather ask?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| The team chat channel, where anyone can answer | |\n| A direct message to Alex or Courtney | |\n| Saved up for a scheduled conversation | |\n\nThis one is worth being honest about. [The 30-minute\nrule](./when-youre-stuck-or-idle.md) — stuck for half an hour, ask in chat — is the single\nmost load-bearing habit in your first month, and it quietly fails if asking in a channel full\nof people you've just met feels expensive. **If that's the case, we start you in DMs and move\nto the channel when you're ready.** That's a normal path, not a concession.\n\n*(Third row is a fair preference, but it's the one we'd push back on: see\n[`your-week.md`](./your-week.md) — the 1:1 is deliberately not the queue for getting\nunstuck.)*\n\n**How do you like to get feedback?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| In the moment, as it happens | |\n| At the end of the session, all together | |\n| Written down afterwards so I can sit with it | |\n\n**Would you rather be told when you're heading the wrong way, or be allowed to get there and\nwork it out?**\n\n>\n\n**How does it feel to be wrong in front of someone?** Not a character question — a scheduling\none. A lot of this ramp is **predict-then-compare**: you write down what you think the answer\nis, then look. T1-1 asks for a prediction before you open the account, and\n[`04-my-work/what-i-got-wrong/`](../04-my-work/README.md) is a folder you're expected to fill.\nThat mechanic works brilliantly and it does mean being visibly wrong on purpose, fairly often,\nin front of your manager in week one.\n\n>\n\n**If you had too much on, how would we know?** Some people go quiet. Some people ask more\nquestions. Some people say it plainly. Tell us your tell, because the thing we most want to\navoid is a good week disappearing silently.\n\n>\n\n---\n\n## 4 · Pace, and how much room you've actually got\n\n**Which is more true of you?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| I'd rather move fast and go back over things later | |\n| I'd rather go slowly and feel solid before moving on | |\n\n**If you finish something early, would you rather have more work, or go deeper on what you just did?**\n\n>\n\n**How much of your week do you expect to have for the training projects, realistically, once\nmeetings and everything else land?**\n\n>\n\n**Do deadlines help you or stress you?** The project briefs can carry a due date or just a\ndirection — the ladder works either way.\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| Give me a date. I work toward them | |\n| Give me an order and let me pace it | |\n| A date, but I'd like it to be a soft one | |\n\n⚠️ **Whatever you pick, the timebox rule doesn't change:** if a one-hour project is running to\nthree, stop and say so. That's a scoping error on our side, and telling us makes the next\nbrief better. It's in [`when-youre-stuck-or-idle.md`](./when-youre-stuck-or-idle.md) and it's\nthe rule people most often assume doesn't apply to them.\n\n---\n\n## 5 · Meetings, and speaking in them\n\nYou land in five standing meetings in your first fortnight — they're all in\n[`your-week.md`](./your-week.md) — and by day 90 you're presenting the PDC section of the\nSearch Deep Dive. That's a long way in twelve weeks, and the handover points are ours to\nchoose. These answers choose them.\n\n**How do you feel about talking in a room of people you don't know yet?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| Fine. I'd rather join in early than sit silent | |\n| Depends on the room. I'll warm up | |\n| I'd rather listen for a while first | |\n\n**Would you rather be asked something cold in a meeting, or get a heads-up beforehand?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| Cold is fine | |\n| A heads-up, so I can think about it | |\n| A heads-up, and ideally the first few times are rehearsed with Alex | |\n\n**One thing that isn't optional, and why:** the L10 huddle opens with a segue — one personal\ngood-news item and one work one, from everyone, including you, from week one. It's a couple of\nsentences, it's the one contribution you can make on day one without knowing anything, and\nit's how a room of new names learns who you are. Everything *else* about when you speak up is\nnegotiable. See [`the-l10-huddle.md`](../02-learning/the-l10-huddle.md) before your first one.\n\n**Anything about meetings that's worked badly for you before?** Worth saying now, while the\ncalendar is still being built.\n\n>\n\n---\n\n## 6 · When and where you do your best work\n\nPractical scheduling, not preference-collecting. Alex sets the paired sessions and the 1:1\nslot, so it costs nothing to put them where they suit you.\n\n**When in the day do you think most clearly?**\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| Early — mornings are my good hours | |\n| Middle of the day | |\n| Later — I get going in the afternoon | |\n| Genuinely doesn't matter | |\n\nThe default assumption baked into your first two weeks is that **the heavy reading goes in the\nmorning and the paired work in the afternoon**. If that's backwards for you, say so and we\nflip the days rather than leave you reading attribution material at your worst hour.\n\n**Would you rather have paired sessions clustered into a couple of days, or spread across the\nweek?**\n\n>\n\n**Anything about your setup, tools or environment that would help?** Screens, note-taking,\nwhere you like to work from — and anything about how you work best that the questions above\ndidn't reach. Open question, and it's fine to leave it blank.\n\n>\n\n---\n\n## 7 · Where you're starting from\n\n**Have you worked with anything industrial, safety-related, healthcare or B2B before?** Not a\nrequirement — it just tells us how much category background to fill in.\n\n>\n\n**Have you worked next to any of these?** Not paid search itself — the disciplines that share\na border with it. Tick anything you've been close to, even secondhand.\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| SEO | |\n| Email or lifecycle marketing | |\n| Ecommerce or merchandising | |\n| Data analysis or reporting of any kind | |\n| Working with an outside agency, or working at one | |\n\nEach of these is a shortcut if you have it. SEO especially — paid and organic at Brady are\ncompeting for the same search terms, which is the thing Meta Monday exists to surface, and\nit's covered in [`who-else-is-searching.md`](../02-learning/who-else-is-searching.md).\n\n**What do you already know about Brady from interviewing here?** Saves us re-explaining things\nyou've already been told three times.\n\n>\n\n---\n\n## 8 · AI tools\n\n**Gemini is Brady's sanctioned AI tool**, and the whole habit is laid out in [the AI\nthread](../03-projects/the-ai-thread.md). This is just to find out where you're starting.\n\n| | ✓ |\n|---|:---:|\n| I use them daily for real work | |\n| I've used them for questions and drafting | |\n| I've tried them and wasn't convinced | |\n| Barely at all | |\n\n**Where do you trust them, and where don't you?**\n\n>\n\n*(For what it's worth, the answer we're building toward is \"trust it to draft, never to know a\nnumber.\" If you're already suspicious of them, you're closer to the right instinct than\nsomeone who isn't.)*\n\n---\n\n## 9 · The two that matter most\n\n**What are you most looking forward to?**\n\n>\n\n**What are you most worried about?** *(This one's also in the baseline — answer it in whichever\nplace you prefer, and it's genuinely useful to know.)*\n\n>\n\n**And one more: what would make day 90 feel like a win to you?** Not the version in the plan —\nyours. If there's something you want to be able to do by then that isn't anywhere in this\nrepo, this is the moment it's cheapest to add.\n\n>\n\n---\n\n## What your answers actually change\n\nEvery row here is a real lever, and pulling it costs us nothing in week one and quite a lot in\nweek six. That's the entire reason for the sheet.\n\n| If you say | What we change |\n|---|---|\n| Reading isn't how you learn | More pairing and shadowing, files demoted to lookup. This is the biggest single change available |\n| You've never used SQL | BigQuery access requested in week one — it's the longest lead time of anything you'll be given |\n| Big spreadsheets aren't second nature | A session on it before Tier 1, rather than mid-project |\n| You grind rather than ask | Alex checks in proactively instead of waiting for a raised hand |\n| Asking in a public channel feels expensive | You start in DMs and move to the channel when you want to. Your daily three lines can go to Alex directly instead of the team channel |\n| You want feedback written down | Project debriefs come back as notes, not just as conversation |\n| Being wrong in front of people is uncomfortable | Predict-then-compare stays, because it's how the learning works — but the early rounds are solo, and Alex goes first on the paired ones |\n| Deadlines help you | Briefs come with dates. If they don't help, they come with an order instead |\n| You'd rather listen in meetings first | You get a heads-up before you're asked anything, and the Deep Dive handover is rehearsed rather than cold |\n| Mornings are your good hours | Heavy reading moves to mornings and paired work to afternoons — or the reverse |\n| You've worked in SEO | Meta Monday becomes a contribution meeting for you much sooner |\n| You've barely used AI tools | The AI thread starts at the beginning and moves at its own pace |\n| There's something you want that isn't in the plan | We look for where it fits. Some of it will fit inside the 90 days, some belongs in [`after-the-ramp.md`](../03-projects/after-the-ramp.md) |\n\n**Anything you disagree with in this table is worth saying too.** If you can see a change we'd\nmake that you'd hate, that's exactly the kind of thing this conversation is for.\n\n---\n\n## What happens with this\n\nYou and Alex talk it through in your first proper conversation. **Some of your answers will\nchange the plan** — the reading order, the pace, how paired sessions run, when we get you into\nBigQuery.\n\n**Then, once, at about day 30: re-read your own answers.** Fifteen minutes, on your own. Some\nof them will look wrong to you by then — you'll have learned things about how this job works\nthat you couldn't have known in week one. Tell Alex which ones changed, and we adjust again.\nThat's the whole follow-up. There isn't a third pass.\n\nOther than that it gets left alone. It isn't scored, it doesn't go into any review, and nobody\nelse needs to see it."
  },
