    body: "# Your week\n\nThe recurring meetings, what each one is for, and what's expected of you in it.\n\n> ⚠️ **Times here are the shape of a normal week, not the authority.** Your calendar invites\n> are. Meetings move — this file is so you know what each one *is* before you walk in.\n\n---\n\n## The shape of it\n\n| Day | | What |\n|-----|---|------|\n| **Mon** | 1:00 | **Meta Monday** — Stuart's digital team weekly (SEO + Email + Paid) |\n| **Tue** | 10:00 | **L10 Huddle** — your team's weekly. The big one |\n| **Wed** | 2:00 | **Seton / EMEDCO weekly** |\n| **Thu** | 10:00 | **Search Deep Dive** — the performance review working session |\n| **Fri** | 9:00–11:00 | **Professional development block** — protected time, not a meeting |\n| Weekly | | **Your 1:1 with Alex** — Alex sets the slot |\n\nMonthly, on top of that: an **Americas Marketing share-out**, a **goal check**, and\n**month-end close**, where the numbers for the previous month get finalized.\n\n---\n\n## Meta Monday · Mon 1:00\n\n**Stuart's whole digital team** — SEO, Email and Paid Media in one room. It frames the week.\n\nThere's no standing agenda; Alex opens a fresh doc about ten minutes beforehand. Things\nflagged to **cascade** out of your team's L10 huddle travel up into this meeting through him.\n\n**Why it matters to you more than it looks:** this is the room where you find out that\n**paid and organic are competing for the same search terms.** Phil's SEO team and your team\nare working the same query space from two directions. You'll build better negatives for\nhaving sat in this meeting for a month.\n\n**What's expected of you:** early on, nothing but attention. Later, contributing the paid\nitems that concern your accounts.\n\n---\n\n## The L10 Huddle · Tue 10:00\n\nYour team's weekly, and the meeting where your work becomes visible. Seven timed segments,\nsame order every week, ending with everyone rating the meeting out of ten.\n\n**It has its own file, and it's worth the read before your first one:**\n[`the-l10-huddle.md`](../02-learning/the-l10-huddle.md).\n\nThe short version of what's expected of you: **bring a segue item from week one** (one\npersonal good-news thing, one work one — that's how the room learns who you are), listen,\nand ask afterward about anything you didn't follow. To-dos of your own start appearing\naround week four.\n\n---\n\n## Seton / EMEDCO weekly · Wed 2:00\n\nThe working session for that account group. You're on a **contribution track** there rather\nthan an ownership one — you'll audit, propose and help rather than own — so treat this one\nas somewhere to learn how a big, busy account group gets run, and to bring the things your\naudits turn up.\n\n---\n\n## Search Deep Dive · Thu 10:00–11:30\n\nThe performance review working session. Monthly, this is where the **Search Deep Dive deck**\ngets built — a rolling trailing-three-month review of paid search and awareness by channel,\npresented to Stuart's team.\n\nIt's also the overflow valve for the L10: anything IDS couldn't finish inside its hour can\ncontinue here with the data in front of everyone, and experiment verdicts get their decisions.\n\n**What's expected of you, by phase:**\n\n| Phase | You |\n|-------|-----|\n| **Days 1–30** | Watch how analysts pull numbers and how the \"why\" gets written |\n| **Days 31–60** | Co-author the PDC section — pull the numbers, draft the explanation |\n| **Days 61–90** | **Own the PDC section end to end** and present it |\n\n> The standard to write toward: a number without a mechanism behind it is noise. \"Revenue\n> was down 8%\" is not a finding. \"Revenue was down 8%, and here's the thing that caused it\"\n> is.\n\n---\n\n## Your 1:1 with Alex · weekly\n\n**This one is yours to run.** Alex's 1:1s are analyst-led, which means you bring the agenda\nand you drive the meeting. He doesn't prepare one for you.\n\nThat's a real adjustment if you've had managers who ran the meeting at you. Two things make\nit easy:\n\n**1. You have a ready-made agenda source.** The L10 app has a per-person 1:1 page: issues\nparked to your 1:1, your to-dos, your rocks, and issues you raised. Open it before the\nmeeting and most of the agenda is already there.\n\n**2. Don't save blockers for it.** If you're stuck on Tuesday, message Alex on Tuesday. The\n1:1 is not the queue for getting unstuck — that's Google Chat, and using it is expected\nrather than tolerated. What the 1:1 is for is the stuff that doesn't fit in a message:\n\n| Bring | Not |\n|-------|-----|\n| Progress and what you learned | A status recital of things already in the to-do list |\n| Decisions you need from Alex | Blockers you've been sitting on since Tuesday |\n| Something structurally confusing you | Tactical questions with a quick answer |\n| What you want to be better at | |\n| Feedback, in both directions | |\n\n**By phase:** at day 30 you're expected to bring an agenda and numbers. By day 60 you're\nrunning it independently.\n\n---\n\n## The Friday professional development block · 9:00–11:00\n\nTwo hours a week, held on the calendar for learning rather than delivery. It is a real block\nand not a euphemism for catch-up time.\n\nUse it on the things that are never urgent: the parts of the fundamentals that didn't land,\na certification, a conference recap someone shared, an account you don't own. **Protect it.**\nIt's the first thing that gets eaten if you let it, and it's the thing that compounds.\n\n---\n\n## The two habits that matter more than any meeting\n\n1. **The daily search-terms review** — about ten minutes, every business day. It is the one\n   daily habit and it's where most of your early judgment gets built.\n2. **Asking in chat the moment you're stuck**, rather than waiting for a scheduled slot.\n\nEverything above is scaffolding around those two."
  },
