    body: "# When you're stuck, blocked, or have nothing to do\n\nRead this once in week one. Come back to it the first time a day goes sideways.\n\n---\n\n## The thing nobody tells a new person\n\n**Some of your first two weeks will fall apart, and it will not be your fault.**\n\nAccess won't be ready. A session will get moved because something caught fire. Alex will get\npulled into a meeting and the thing you were going to do together doesn't happen. You'll\nfinish the reading faster than the plan assumed and it's 2pm on a Wednesday with nothing\nin front of you.\n\nEvery one of those is normal, all of them are *our* planning problem rather than your\nperformance problem, and none of them is a reason to sit quietly and hope it resolves.\n\n**The failure mode we actually worry about is a conscientious person losing two days rather\nthan saying \"I'm stuck.\"** That is the one thing that would genuinely disappoint us. Nothing\nelse on this page comes close.\n\n---\n\n## The rules\n\n| Situation | What to do |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| **Stuck on something for 30 minutes** | Ask. In chat. That's the whole rule. Thirty minutes of your time is worth more than the two minutes it costs someone to answer |\n| **Blocked on someone else** | Say so in chat *and* say who you're waiting on. Then pick something off the list below — don't wait idle |\n| **Half a day with nothing to do** | Say that too. \"I've finished X and I'm not sure what's next\" is a completely normal sentence and it is information Alex needs |\n| **Alex is unavailable** | **Courtney** is your peer buddy — day-to-day questions and second opinions are explicitly hers as well as his. You are not interrupting her |\n| **You think the plan is wrong** | Say it. You have the only fresh pair of eyes anyone will have on this material for a year |\n\n> **On timeboxes:** if a one-hour project is running to three, that's a scoping error on our\n> side, not a failure on yours. Stop at the hour, say where you got to, and flag it. That\n> feedback makes the next project better. Grinding silently makes nothing better.\n\n---\n\n## Things you can always do alone\n\nNo permission needed, no one else required, useful in any order. Pick one when you're blocked\nor ahead.\n\n### Needs account access\n\n1. **Tour an account you don't own.** Open Seton US or EMEDCO read-only and try the T1-1\n   questions on it. You'll be doing this properly later — arriving with opinions is a head\n   start.\n2. **A keep/kill dry run.** Pull yesterday's search terms on any account, make your own\n   calls on 20 of them, and keep the list. When you do it for real you'll have a baseline\n   to compare against — that's the predict-then-compare mechanic, self-served.\n3. **Find something that looks wrong.** Anything. A campaign spending with no conversions, a\n   name that doesn't match the convention, an ad group with one keyword. You don't need to\n   be right — bring it as a question and you'll learn something either way.\n\n### Needs nothing at all\n\n4. **Work your questions file.** Go through\n   [`04-my-work/questions/`](../04-my-work/README.md) and try to answer your own oldest\n   questions from the learning files. Note which ones you *can't* answer — that list is\n   genuinely valuable to Alex.\n5. **Re-read [`how-brady-measures.md`](../02-learning/how-brady-measures.md).** It reads\n   differently every time for the first month. That isn't wasted time; it's the single\n   highest-consequence thing in the repo.\n6. **Write the thing you just learned in your own words.** If you can't, you haven't got it\n   yet — and finding that out now is cheap.\n7. **Read a project brief that's further ahead** than where you are. You won't do it yet, but\n   knowing what's coming makes the current one make more sense.\n8. **Improve this repo.** Something in here will be unclear, out of date or plain wrong.\n   Fixing it is real work and it's the one contribution only a new person can make — in six\n   months you won't be able to see the problems any more.\n\n---\n\n## If access isn't working\n\nThis is the most common way a first week goes wrong, and it's worth being pushy about\nbecause access takes days to fix and minutes to request.\n\n**By the end of day one you should be able to open:** Google Ads (and see your accounts),\nthe MTD Spend dashboard, Google Chat, your calendar with the standing meetings on it, and\nthis repo.\n\n**By the end of week two, add:** Microsoft/Bing Ads, Power BI, Adobe Analytics and Jira.\nBigQuery usually takes longest because it goes through a different team — **if it hasn't\nbeen requested by the end of week one, ask Alex about it specifically.** It's needed later\nthan the others but it has the longest lead time, so it's the one that quietly bites.\n\n> **What each of these tools is *for*** — which one owns which number, and why two disagree — is\n> [the tools thread](../03-projects/the-tools-thread.md). This list gets you the logins; that\n> file tells you what to do once you're in.\n\n**If something on that list doesn't work: say so the same day, not at your next 1:1.** Nobody\nwill think you're complaining. Waiting a week to mention it is the only version of this that\ncauses a problem.\n\n---\n\n## One thing to hold on to\n\nBeing confused for the first month is the job working correctly, not you doing it badly. The\nplan expects you to be confused — that's why nothing you touch in month one can break\nanything.\n\nThe only way to get this wrong is to be confused *quietly*."
